{
  "dom_id": "step-q46382",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The dimensions of the cover of a chemistry book are $28\\,\\text{cm}\\times 14\\,\\text{cm}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ Rounded to the nearest square centimeter, what is the smallest possible area of the book's cover?",
  "worked_solution": "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{cm}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the book's cover. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 28\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=27.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & 14\\,\\text{cm}-0.5\\,\\text{cm}=13.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the smallest possible area of the book's cover is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =27.5\\,\\text{cm}\\times 13.5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =371.25\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈371\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square centimeter.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
